{"astp_version": "0.1.0", "event_type": "turn", "session": {"id": "s-9ac733fd684e", "start_time": "2026-09-22T10:38:34.226Z", "user_id": "u-claims-0417", "tenant_id": "thornfield-mutual", "client_app": "claims-copilot", "agent_id": "astp-lab-agent-1", "config_version": "0.2.0"}, "turn": {"index": 0, "timestamp": "2026-09-22T10:38:43.405Z", "model_id": "claude-haiku-4-5", "model_version": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001", "tokens_in": 13375, "tokens_out": 104, "latency": 9.155, "finish_reason": "tool_use"}, "content": {"prompt_text": "What guidance do we have on subsidence claims, and when should a structural engineer be instructed?", "prompt_hash": "sha256:e73401e2ca3b9a7a39aae89f6ddef5d38c5c786a60ffd906c126deebdf40ca02", "response_text": "I'll search our documentation for guidance on subsidence claims and when to instruct a structural engineer.", "response_hash": "sha256:e2df8e3786f58c53a48a35e9e1b2112d37d50e92bf66a137868564cd16ab270f", "system_prompt_version": "sp-0.2.0", "redaction_applied": false}, "control": {"canary_triggered": false, "policy_version": "0.1.0", "block_reason": null}}
{"astp_version": "0.1.0", "event_type": "turn", "session": {"id": "s-9ac733fd684e", "start_time": "2026-09-22T10:38:34.226Z", "user_id": "u-claims-0417", "tenant_id": "thornfield-mutual", "client_app": "claims-copilot", "agent_id": "astp-lab-agent-1", "config_version": "0.2.0"}, "turn": {"index": 1, "timestamp": "2026-09-22T10:38:43.677Z", "model_id": "claude-haiku-4-5", "model_version": "claude-haiku-4-5-20251001", "tokens_in": 14827, "tokens_out": 335, "latency": 0.2715, "finish_reason": "end_turn"}, "content": {"prompt_text": "What guidance do we have on subsidence claims, and when should a structural engineer be instructed?", "prompt_hash": "sha256:e73401e2ca3b9a7a39aae89f6ddef5d38c5c786a60ffd906c126deebdf40ca02", "response_text": "Based on our documentation, here's the guidance on subsidence claims:\n\n## First Response\n\nWithin five working days of notification, you should:\n\n1. **Appoint a subsidence engineer from the panel** — this is the key instruction point\n2. Ask the policyholder for photographs of every crack (with a rule or coin for scale) and dates first noticed\n3. Establish whether any tree is within a distance equal to its mature height, and record the species\n4. Confirm whether the property has been underpinned before and obtain any previous engineer's report\n\n(prc-subsidence-005#c00)\n\n## When the Engineer Reports\n\nThe engineer's first report will give a provisional cause and recommend either monitoring or immediate action. If monitoring is recommended, the standard period is **twelve months** to observe seasonal ground movement across a full cycle — shorter periods give unreliable results (prc-subsidence-005#c01).\n\n## Key Points\n\n- Site investigation includes trial pits, soil sampling and drainage tests\n- **Drainage defects are common and cheaper to remedy than structural work**, so test drains early\n- If vegetation is implicated and the tree is on third party land, write to the owner with evidence\n- Underpinning is a last resort — used only where removing the cause won't allow recovery\n- Contact the policyholder at least monthly\n\nNote that subsidence claims carry a £1,000 excess under Section 1 of the Thornfield Household policy (pol-home-002#c00).", "response_hash": "sha256:d05c1d8c667e3b2c715d86118ac8b227044510399f2f4d5fb4212e9f821eebca", "system_prompt_version": "sp-0.2.0", "redaction_applied": false}, "control": {"canary_triggered": false, "policy_version": "0.1.0", "block_reason": null}}
